WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:12.330
We’re going to let you in on a little secret.

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:17.790
One that’s maybe not actually that much
of a secret: sometimes, we don’t know what

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:19.119
we’re looking at.

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:24.730
We’ll find a microbe that looks familiar,
but the name of it eludes us.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:29.420
Fortunately, we don’t need to know the name
of something to show it off.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:34.710
So when these situations arrive, we’ll go
with a name that is as specific as we can

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:40.980
get, like the ciliate here that we refer to
so specifically as…”ciliate.”

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:45.730
But with our new differential interference
contrast microscope, we’ve been able to

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:51.129
make out all sorts of new details that we
couldn’t see before.

00:00:51.129 --> 00:00:56.840
And in some cases, those new details have
helped us identify organisms that we could

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:58.120
not name before.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:04.180
Like if you look closer here, you might be
able to make out this little bit of a crystalline

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:05.180
organelle.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:10.840
That little tiny thing is called the organelle
of Lieberkühn, though it’s also known as

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:15.200
the watch-glass organelle because it resembles
a watch glass.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.940
What does it do?

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:21.939
We’re not completely sure, though it might
be something related to phototaxis, which

00:01:21.939 --> 00:01:25.240
is how you move toward or away from light.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:31.180
But what it helps us do is identify what we’re
looking at and give this ciliate the more

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:34.460
specific name of Ophyroglena.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:41.640
Ophyroglena is a genus of histophagous ciliates,
meaning that they feed on tissue.

00:01:41.659 --> 00:01:46.299
The one we’re watching here has been drawn
to this crustacean like a shark smelling a

00:01:46.299 --> 00:01:52.350
drop of blood in water, except it’s a microbe
drawn to a crack in its shell.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:58.960
And once it gets inside, the ophryglena gorges
on the tissue inside.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.040
So the Ophyroglena seems like it should be
easy enough to find.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:08.190
We just need to keep an eye out for the watch-glass
organelle and other dead organisms.

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:13.830
But one of the reasons it’s hard to identify
these organisms is because they do not always

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:15.630
look like this.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:22.160
Some ciliates have life cycles that take them
through different shapes, and sizes, and activities,

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:23.470
and responses.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:28.840
It may be the same organism, but the way that
it looks or behaves changes.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:36.160
Ophyroglena’s life cycle is made up of 5
established stages, as well as a few additional,

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:39.180
though more rare, possibilities.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:46.080
The organism starts in its theront stage,
taking on an elongated shape as it swims freely

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.440
about the microcosmos.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:52.580
It’s moving a bit quickly, so it might not
be very apparent, but at this point the watch-glass

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:54.860
organelle should be visible.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:03:00.690
The theront stage doesn’t have food vacuoles
yet, but it still seeks food.

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:06.040
It’ll swim around for about two days chasing
chemical stimuli and light that point it in

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:08.490
the general direction of food.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:14.690
But once they settle down into a more restful
state, they begin to fill up with food vacuoles

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:17.430
and move on to their next stage.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:23.570
The Ophyroglena becomes more opaque as it
transitions into its equivalent of adulthood:

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:25.660
the trophont stage.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:31.010
At this point, the swimming trophont will
still be drawn to light, perhaps to help hunt

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:35.220
down the same dead tissue these bacteria are
attracted to.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:42.170
But as trophont gives way to protomont, the
Ophyroglena starts to change as well.

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:48.180
Some will still be attracted to light, but
others will be more drawn to an absence of it.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:52.730
This one got stuck between some glass, but
with a little water added to the slide, it’s

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:53.960
back to moving.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:59.470
Then after a few hours of eating, the protomont
will begin to slow down.

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:06.010
This is the protomont’s time for change,
and so it morphs into a tomont.

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:12.100
As a tomont, the Ophyroglena avoids the light
as it prepares to reproduce.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:18.530
Some Ophyroglena species form a division cyst,
while others create and stay attached to a

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:21.630
sticky substance instead.

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:27.969
Whatever the venue, the result is the same:
the tomont divides several times to produce

00:04:27.969 --> 00:04:36.599
anywhere from 4 to 8 smaller and rounder tomites
whose watch-glass organelle has now disappeared.

00:04:36.599 --> 00:04:42.819
The tomites might form a chain that slowly
rotates for a few hours until it separates

00:04:42.819 --> 00:04:45.740
into theronts, bringing the cycle full circle.

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:50.770
At times, researchers have observed deviations
from this typical cycle.

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:55.849
In some cases, the tomont might enter what’s
been termed the “migratory stage,” which

00:04:55.849 --> 00:05:01.289
is noted for the largeness of the microbe
and agitated movement before dividing into

00:05:01.289 --> 00:05:05.560
tomites or, in rarer cases, forming a cyst.

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:10.990
It’s not clear what conditions would cause
the Ophyroglena to enter this stage, just

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:14.599
that it’s a thing that happens sometimes.

00:05:14.599 --> 00:05:20.870
Now, you might be thinking to yourself, “Boy,
we seem to understand all this really well.”

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:27.240
And honestly, the many lives of the Ophyroglena
would be its own business and we would probably

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:32.419
not know all of these things if it weren’t
for the fish problem.

00:05:32.419 --> 00:05:39.300
In 1959, scientists at the Eastern Fish Disease
Laboratory at the U.S. Bureau of Sport Fisheries

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:45.610
and Wildlife reported that several of the
fish in their aquarium had died soon after

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:47.810
being infected with a ciliate.

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:54.229
The epithelium of the infected fish had sloughed
off and been marked with white spots.

00:05:54.229 --> 00:05:59.840
With the right lighting and background, the
researchers could even see a mass of ciliates

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:02.099
swarming around the fish.

00:06:02.099 --> 00:06:09.139
In their descriptions of the ciliate, you
can see the hallmarks of the Ophyroglena life cycle.

00:06:09.139 --> 00:06:13.470
When active and feeding, the culprit had an
elongated shape.

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:18.639
And after dining on their course of fish,
they would become more rounded and descend

00:06:18.639 --> 00:06:21.139
to the bottom of the aquarium.

00:06:21.139 --> 00:06:25.310
This fish-feeding frenzy does not seem to
be typical of Ophyroglena.

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:31.319
At the very least, this report is the only
one we’ve found of its kind, though they

00:06:31.319 --> 00:06:34.660
do have a documented history of infecting
insects.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:41.610
But in the case of the fish infection, the
researchers compared Ophyroglena to the obligate

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:50.050
parasite Ichthyophthirius multifiliis, better
known as Ich or just Ich disease in fish.

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:52.639
The symptoms of both of these infections are
similar.

00:06:52.639 --> 00:06:59.650
And Ichthyopththirius is a ciliate whose life
cycle has many steps in common with Ophyroglena,

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:01.680
including tomites.

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:07.939
The life cycle of Ich disease is formatted
around the fish the parasite must infect to survive.

00:07:07.939 --> 00:07:14.449
And as such, the ways of fighting it are also
oriented around interfering with this process

00:07:14.449 --> 00:07:17.800
of change and growth in the microbe itself.

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:23.969
Ophyroglena may not have the same infectious
mandate, but the parallels between their lives

00:07:23.969 --> 00:07:29.559
and that of other disease-causing ciliates
shows why understanding life cycles isn’t

00:07:29.559 --> 00:07:34.070
just about how an organism changes over the
course of its own lifetime.

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:42.069
It’s also about how those changes in turn
affect the world around them, as we all, even

00:07:42.069 --> 00:07:47.110
the small of us undoubtedly do.

00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:52.059
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:07:52.059 --> 00:07:56.160
And thank you, as always, to these people
